const e="penticton-kiwanis-speech-arts",d=2025,t="pkmf.org returns HTTP 403 or resets the connection from this machine, so the pages were read through the Internet Archive (snapshots from May to July 2025: Categories & Classes, Ages Fees & Time Limits, Speech Arts home, General Rules & Regulations, Provincials, Awards, Scholarships, Registration, Contact, About). The current edition's registration dates, venue and adjudicator are not printed (the registration page says information will be posted in September; the Adjudicators, Venue and Calendar pages were empty), so dates are past-edition Variables marked with their year. The General Rules page still prints the 2024 registration dates (opened 1 December 2023, closed 29 January 2024), and the Speech Arts home page still prints an old 2018 block (11 and 12 April 2018, Leir House) beside the current rules text; only the current rules text is used as rules. Newer value used: the Ages, Fees page prints Duos and Trios at CAD 20 per person; the template's evidence quotes CAD 15 per person from an earlier version. Conflict kept: the General Rules say cups, trophies and medallions go to marks of 85 or over, the Scholarships page says marks of 80% or more; the festival's costume rule allows costumes only in Drama and Shakespeare, the Literary Monologue class allows simple costumes; the provincial Public Speaking and Storytelling age limits differ between the festival class page (Intermediate 17 and under, Senior 22 and under) and the Provincials page (16 and under, 23 and under). The Adult group is printed as 23 and over with a Seniors 55+ sub-tier; the template splits it at 54. Classes the template does not carry (Humorous, Performance, Canadian Poetry, Two Voices, Trio or Quartet, Sonnet, I Like This Poem, Creative Storytelling, Sight Reading, Trio/Group drama, Shakespeare Duo and Trio/Group, Mime Duo/Trio/Group, Concert Group, Public Speaking and Storytelling, Impromptu, Choric Drama, Readers' Theatre, Workshop Class) have one line each under 'other'. Not printed in the pages read: marks scale or criteria, scholarship amounts, current-year dates, venue and adjudicator, a registration link address, and the sponsors' names (not copied). Winner names and donor names on the awards pages were not copied; trophy and award names were kept.",o=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"Penticton Kiwanis Music, Dance and Speech Arts Festival - Speech and Dramatic Arts. Read these rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Entries are made online only. Write the competitor's age on the entry form. Teachers and competitors must check that the student and the selection are eligible; accepting an entry does not make it eligible.","Notes":["Age is counted on 31 December before the upcoming festival.","All disciplines run at the festival's choice, and depend on enough entries.","These rules apply to the Penticton festival only. Provincial rules vary.","{{RegistrationNotice}}"],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules","Items":["All matters that these rules do not cover go to the Festival Committee. The decision of the Festival Committee is final and binding on everyone.","Competitors and teachers must follow the Rules & Regulations of the festival. Entries are the responsibility of competitors and teachers. The festival is not responsible for errors or missing items.","All selections must be memorized unless a class says otherwise.","The language and content of every selection must be suitable for an all-ages mixed audience.","Classes are open to students of all ages. Any entry may be performed for adjudication only. Write 'non-competitive' on the entry form.","Timing begins with the first word and ends with the last word. It does not include the announcement of the title or author, a spoken introduction, or applause. Competitors must state an accurate time on the entry form. Time limits must be kept where they are set.","Costumes and props are allowed only for Drama selections and Shakespeare classes. They must be part of the selection. Choose material that needs the fewest props. The props must be easy to carry. Competitors must be able to set them up quickly and without help. Live flame is not allowed on stage.","A class may be divided or combined at the choice of the Festival Committee.","Competitors are sorted into the age groups below. The groups are subdivided as needed: Non-competitive Primary, Junior, Intermediate, Senior, Adult and Seniors 55+.","No competitor may use the festival performance area to practise while that discipline is at the venue. A competitor who does this may be disqualified.","Commercial advertising for the purpose of soliciting must be approved by the Festival Committee before it is shown in any festival hall.","Additional rules may apply in specific disciplines. Check the syllabus."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["All competitions are open to amateurs only, except classes that are open to all. An amateur is a person whose main income does not come from music or dance services. This does not apply to conductors of choirs, bands or orchestras, or to bona fide students who teach to pay for their own education. Say on the registration if a member of a group is a professional.","Competitors must be bona fide residents of the Interior of British Columbia, or of Northern British Columbia east of Chilliwack, or of the Okanogan District of Washington State (from the southern border of the Wenatchee District north along the Columbia River to Brewster, then north along the Okanagan River to the border). People from other areas are welcome but get adjudication only. They cannot get awards or represent the festival at the Provincials.","Where an age limit is set, the entry form must be signed by a parent, guardian or teacher in the space provided.","Age is counted on 31 December before the upcoming festival. Write the age on the entry form.","No person may compete in two different entries in the same class.","No competitor may choose and perform the same piece in more than one class in the same discipline.","A competitor or group that repeats a winning entry in any class within five years gets adjudication only.","A child aged 6 and under who wants to compete, or to enter classes other than the non-competitive ones, must enter as a Junior and pay the Junior fee.","Non-competitive Primary (6 years and under) and Seniors 55+ are non-competitive. They get adjudication only.","All Music and Speech and Dramatic Arts competitors must enter at least two classes to qualify for any award.","Only competitors aged 20 or younger on 31 December before the festival, who meet the scholarship requirements, can win major scholarships.","Each competitor must bring the book that holds the original selection to the competition, and give it to the adjudicator's secretary at least 15 minutes before the class starts. The book is the proof of copyright.","The entry form must give the title, author and name of the publication. The selected material must be marked clearly in the published copy: page, and start and finish lines.","Each competitor must also give a typed copy of the selection to the adjudicator, labelled with the student's name, age and class name. The copies are destroyed after the class. The competitor must give the festival a typed copy or outline of the selection.","Photocopied material cannot replace the original copy, unless it is approved through CanCopy or by a publisher's letter. An electronic version of the published material is accepted if receipts show it was bought."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register","Items":["Entries may only be made online.","Registration for the 2024 festival opened on {{RegistrationOpens}} and closed on {{RegistrationCloses}}. The website now says the next registration information will be posted in September.","Pay by PayPal or by mailing a cheque to PKMF. All entry fees are due {{FeesDueWithin}}.","You get confirmation of the entry, and the date and place of the classes, by email when you register.","The Committee may refuse any entry without giving a reason. If it does, the fee is refunded.","Teachers and competitors must check the eligibility of the student and of the selection. Accepting an entry does not make it eligible. The Festival Committee is not responsible for wrong or unsuitable entries in the official program.","An entry fee cannot be returned when the competitor withdraws. Medical emergencies are reviewed one by one.","After the official program is published, requests to change the schedule are refused.","A competitor may ask the Festival Committee to change a category on an existing entry only until the date to be announced. A fee of {{FeeCategoryChange}} must come with the request, and it must be received before the performance.","Teachers can log in on the registration page to make bulk payments.","For information about Speech and Dramatic Arts, contact the discipline captain: {{SpeechArtsCaptain}}."]},{"Key":"fees","Title":"Entry Fees","Items":["Solo classes: Non-competitive Primary (6 years and under) {{FeeNonCompPrimary}}. Junior (7 to 13 years) {{FeeJunior}}. Intermediate (14 to 17 years) {{FeeIntermediate}}. Senior (18 to 22 years) {{FeeSenior}}. Adult (23 years and over) {{FeeAdult}}. Seniors (55 and over) {{FeeSeniors55}}.","Group classes (all ages): Duos {{FeeDuo}}. Trios {{FeeTrio}}. Groups of 4 or more {{FeeGroup}}.","Sightreading, all ages: {{FeeSightreading}}.","Solo recital classes: Concert Group (age 7 and up) {{FeeConcertGroup}}. Story + Public Speech {{FeeStoryPublicSpeech}}.","Check each class for its time limit. Please check the syllabus descriptions for time limits."]},{"Key":"dates","Title":"Dates","Items":["The festival website printed no dates for the next Speech and Dramatic Arts event. The registration page says the information will be posted in September.","Past edition: the Speech and Dramatic Arts classes were on {{PastSpeechArtsDates}}."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Venue","Items":["The Venue page of the festival website printed no venue for the next event.","Past edition: the Speech and Dramatic Arts classes were held at {{PastSpeechArtsVenue}}.","Mailing address: {{FestivalMailingAddress}}."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Provincials","Items":["Speech Arts classes that count for the provincial finals (Performing Arts BC, PABC) are poetry, sonnet (senior level only), prose, drama and Shakespeare.","Students aged 11 to 22 who want to be considered for the provincial finals should enter Concert Group and at least one other qualifying class.","Public Speaking and Storytelling is a separate provincial category. Students aged 11 to 22 may be recommended from PKMF without taking other speech arts classes. They must enter this class in the Penticton festival to be eligible.","Each regional festival may send one representative for every 100 registrants in each age group to the B.C. Provincial Finals. Age and grade levels (Speech and Dramatic Arts grade, not school grade) are counted on 31 December before the Provincial Festival.","Provincial categories: Junior, ages 11 to 13, minimum grade level 6, time limit 15 minutes. Intermediate, 17 years and under, minimum grade 7, 17 minutes. Senior, 22 years and under, minimum grade 8, 20 minutes.","Provincial Public Speaking and Storytelling needs one selection of each, with time limits: Junior (11 to 13 years) 12 minutes. Intermediate (the provincials page says 16 years and under) 14 minutes. Senior (the provincials page says 23 years and under) 16 minutes.","The provincial Public Speaking outline must include: Purpose, Central Idea, Intended Audience, Introduction, Body and Conclusion. The Storytelling selection must include a very short plot outline with the key events of the story.","Speech and Dramatic Arts participants at the Provincial festival may also take part in the Shakespeare classes.","For the definitions of Prose, Drama and Poetry at the provincial level, ask the Speech Arts Captain or see the provincial syllabus at www.bcprovincials.com. For Provincial Excellence and Canada West Festival eligibility, see the PABC syllabus.","Past provincial dates: Performing Arts BC Festival {{PastProvincialsPABC}}. Canada West Festival {{PastProvincialsCanWest}}."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging","Items":["All competitors in competitive classes get an adjudication and a mark for each performance.","The adjudicator's decision in all matters of adjudication is final. The adjudicator may stop any performance at will. The festival board, following the syllabus, has the final decision if needed.","Competitors are named to the adjudicator by name and number. They must not start before the adjudicator gives the signal.","If an infringement has occurred, no mark is given, but an adjudication is given.","The presiding captain may ask any competitor to certify that the regulations have been met."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Marks and Certificates","Items":["Certificates go to competitors who place first, second and third in a class with a mark of 80 or more.","A competitor who gets a mark of 80 or more and is the only competitor in the class gets a First place certificate.","Second and Third places are given if there is a second or third competitor in the class who reaches 80 or more.","Ties for Second and Third place are allowed. Ties do not happen for First place.","All competitors aged 10 and under who do not get First, Second or Third place get a Participation Certificate. Non-competitive entries aged 10 and under also get a Participation Certificate.","First, Second and Third place certificates go to each person in a duo, trio or quartet. Only one certificate goes to a group of five or more competitors (music and speech arts)."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Awards, Trophies and Scholarships","Items":["Competitors with a mark of 80% or more can be eligible for medallions, trophies and awards. The General Rules page says cups, trophies and medallions go to competitors who get 85 or over and meet any set requirements.","In Speech Arts, medallions go to all students who get a mark of 90 or more. Medallions are kept by the winners.","Trophies go to the competitor with the highest mark in the named category, if the mark is 80% or more. A competitor must enter at least three solo classes to be eligible for a solo trophy. Keeper trophies belong to the winner.","Winners of major and money awards do not also get a medallion for the same competition piece (music and speech arts).","Trophies, shields and cups that are competed for each year belong to the Okanagan Valley Music Festival Society, unless shown otherwise.","Speech Arts scholarships: Junior, Intermediate and Senior.","Speech Arts awards: Adjudicator's Choice Encouragement Award (Junior and Intermediate). Top Mark awards (Junior and Intermediate/Senior) for Poetry classes, Prose classes, Drama classes, Shakespeare, Mime, Public Speaking and Storytelling. Duo's Top Mark (all ages).","Speech Arts trophies: the Nina Grigor Trophy (Poetry Solo) and the Speech and Dramatic Arts Trophy (Best Duo, Trio, Group).","The Scholarship for Speech and Dramatic Arts is for candidates aged 13 and older who enter three selections from Poetry (all forms), Prose, Dramatic Scene, Public Speaking, Shakespeare Solo or a Concert Group.","PKMF Tri-Discipline Scholarship: for a young person registered in three or more solo entry disciplines of the festival (any three of Classical or Modern Dance, Stage Dance, Classical Voice, Musical Theatre, Pianoforte, Speech Arts, Strings, Woodwind, Brass or Guitar; Classical Dance and Modern Dance count as one). Apply by {{ScholarshipDeadline}} to {{FestivalMailingAddress}} or by email to {{FestivalOffice}}, marked for the PKMF Scholarship Panel. The application gives the name, the address, a list of confirmed entries (discipline, category or title, class number) and a short essay of 250 words or less on what the performing arts mean to you. Winners are chosen by a panel of board members and presented at the Highlights Concert.","At the choice of the Festival Committee, scholarships and awards may not all be awarded. Award requirements can change from year to year, so read the syllabus and program."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Protests","Items":["Protests must be made in writing to the Festival Coordinator with a protest fee of {{ProtestFee}}.","No inquiries may be sent directly to the adjudicator."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct","Items":["Talking to the adjudicators is forbidden for competitors, teachers and parents, except through the Music Festival Committee. A person who does this is disqualified.","The Committee respects the right of every competitor to a performance without distractions or interruptions. People who are not courteous to the competitor are removed from the venue, and the Committee may take disciplinary action.","Competitors must not take pictures during a performance. This can be distracting and can lead to disciplinary action, including disqualification.","Audience: arrive on time. Turn off phones, or put them on vibrate. Do not speak during the performance, not even whisper, except in an emergency. Stay seated for the whole performance, and leave only during a break. Do not eat or drink in the theatre. Do not put your feet on the seats or kick the seat in front. Do not throw anything on the stage. Applaud when it is right to do so and when the performance ends. Do not whistle or scream at the performers. Keep silent after the bell and the introduction.","Copies of music and adjudication sheets must be collected right after the class. No certificates are mailed. The festival is not responsible for music left by competitors."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Other Classes and Disciplines","Items":["Humorous Poetry: any poem of a light or humorous kind. 4 minutes.","Performance Poetry: a poem written to be performed before an audience. Movement is allowed. Language must be fit for all ages. 3 minutes.","Canadian Poetry: any poem on any theme by a Canadian poet. 3 minutes.","Poetry for Two Voices: a poem arranged for two people. 5 minutes. Poetry for Trio or Quartet: a poem arranged for three or four people. 5 minutes.","Sonnet / Sonnet Sequence: a sonnet or two linked sonnets with a short commentary on the connection. 4 minutes in total. This is a senior-level provincial class.","I Like This Poem: any poem performed in a style that suits its form. 3 minutes.","Creative Storytelling: the adjudicator gives a picture and the competitor makes up a story, after one minute to prepare. 2 minutes.","Sight Reading: a prose or poetry passage suited to the age or speech grade level, read aloud after 60 seconds of study. Competitors wait outside the hall until their turn. No time limit is printed.","Trio/Group drama: a scene for 3 to 8 characters from a published play or dramatized story. 10 minutes.","Shakespeare Duo: a scene for two actors from a published play by Shakespeare. 6 minutes. Shakespeare Trio/Group: a scene for 3 to 8 actors. 10 minutes.","Mime Duo/Trio/Group: a silent scene by 2 to 8 players. Chairs, stools or benches may be used, no other props. 6 minutes.","Concert Group (recital class, a provincial class): ages 7 to 10, any three speech arts selections as one continuous concert, titles and authors announced only, 8 minutes. Junior, ages 11 to 13: one selection of poetry, prose and drama, 15 minutes. Intermediate, 17 years and under: the same, 17 minutes. Senior, 22 years and under: poetry, prose, drama and sonnet joined by a theme, with commentary, 20 minutes. Primary, Junior and Intermediate students announce titles and authors and may add a short commentary. Drama must have commentary to set the scene. Costume changes and props must be handled without leaving the playing area.","Public Speaking and Storytelling (recital class): one original Public Speaking selection and one Storytelling selection. Junior (11 to 13 years) 12 minutes at most. Intermediate (17 years and under) 14 minutes. Senior (22 years and under) 16 minutes.","Impromptu Public Speaking: speak for 2 to 4 minutes on a subject from the adjudicator, after 5 minutes to prepare. 4 minutes at most.","Choric Drama: 10 to 30 entrants, the chorus has a major role, lines memorized, one selection, set-up in under 5 minutes, a leader may conduct but may not speak. 10 minutes.","Readers' Theatre: a group of at most 12 readers who may read from the script, no costumes, the adjudicator gets the published and a copied version. 15 minutes.","Workshop Class: a group class with the Speech Arts adjudicator. The website says to check back for more information.","The festival also runs Music, Choral, Classical Voice, Instrumental/Strings, Musical Theatre, Pianoforte and Dance. The General Rules also cover music: open music is handed to the adjudicator secretary, music must be numbered every five bars and labelled, recorded accompaniment is not allowed in any voice class except choir and the popular instrumental class, and copied music needs written permission. These disciplines are not in this template."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Url":"http://www.pkmf.org/speech-arts-syllabus.aspx"},{"Title":"Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits","Url":"http://www.pkmf.org/speech-arts-fees.aspx"},{"Title":"Speech Arts Provincials","Url":"http://www.pkmf.org/speech-arts-provincials.aspx"},{"Title":"Speech Arts Scholarships & Awards","Url":"http://www.pkmf.org/speech-arts-awards.aspx"},{"Title":"General Rules & Regulations","Url":"http://www.pkmf.org/rules.aspx"},{"Title":"Registration","Url":"http://www.pkmf.org/registration.aspx"}]}`),a=[],i=[{AgeGroup:"NonComp Primary",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits; PKMF Speech Arts Home",Sections:{About:["Non-competitive Primary 'Lollipop League': 6 years and under. A student of ages 4 to 6 is in this group."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Entries get an adjudication only, with no mark.","A child aged 6 or under who wants to compete must enter as a Junior and pay the Junior fee."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Non-competitive Primary 'Lollipop League': 6 years and under. A student of ages 4 to 6 is in this group.

## Rules
- Entries get an adjudication only, with no mark.
- A child aged 6 or under who wants to compete must enter as a Junior and pay the Junior fee.`},{AgeGroup:"Junior (7-13)",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits",Sections:{About:["Junior: ages 7 to 13. The group is subdivided as needed."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["Competitive: each performance gets an adjudication and a mark, and is eligible for certificates, cups and scholarships."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Junior: ages 7 to 13. The group is subdivided as needed.

## Judging
- Competitive: each performance gets an adjudication and a mark, and is eligible for certificates, cups and scholarships.`},{AgeGroup:"Intermediate",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits",Sections:{About:["Intermediate: ages 14 to 17. The group is subdivided as needed."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["Competitive: each performance gets an adjudication and a mark, and is eligible for certificates, cups and scholarships."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Intermediate: ages 14 to 17. The group is subdivided as needed.

## Judging
- Competitive: each performance gets an adjudication and a mark, and is eligible for certificates, cups and scholarships.`},{AgeGroup:"Senior (18-22)",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits",Sections:{About:["Senior: ages 18 to 22. The group is subdivided as needed."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:["Competitive: each performance gets an adjudication and a mark, and is eligible for certificates, cups and scholarships."],Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Senior: ages 18 to 22. The group is subdivided as needed.

## Judging
- Competitive: each performance gets an adjudication and a mark, and is eligible for certificates, cups and scholarships.`},{AgeGroup:"Adult (23-54)",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits",Sections:{About:["Adult: a student of any level aged 23 and older."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:null,Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Adult: a student of any level aged 23 and older.`},{AgeGroup:"Seniors (55+)",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits; PKMF Speech Arts Home",Sections:{About:["Seniors: ages 55 and older, with their own lower fee."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Seniors 55+ is a non-competitive group. Entries get an adjudication only."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Seniors: ages 55 and older, with their own lower fee.

## Rules
- Seniors 55+ is a non-competitive group. Entries get an adjudication only.`},{AgeGroup:"Group Classes",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits",Sections:{About:["Duo, trio and group classes are for all ages and are not divided by age."],TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Duo and trio fees are paid per person. A group of 4 or more pays one flat fee."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## About this competition
- Duo, trio and group classes are for all ages and are not divided by age.

## Rules
- Duo and trio fees are paid per person. A group of 4 or more pays one flat fee.`}],s=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Lyric Poetry","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A short, unified poem that expresses the poet's own experience and emotion. Present it in a relaxed way with limited movement. Examples printed: Solitude by Bliss Carman; To Autumn by Keats; sonnets of Shakespeare and Wordsworth.","In poetry the language is molded into some kind of design. In prose it is not."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 5 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A short, unified poem that expresses the poet's own experience and emotion. Present it in a relaxed way with limited movement. Examples printed: Solitude by Bliss Carman; To Autumn by Keats; sonnets of Shakespeare and Wordsworth.\\n- In poetry the language is molded into some kind of design. In prose it is not.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 5 minutes."},{"Title":"Narrative Poetry","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A poem that tells a story and usually has dialogue. Telling the story is the speaker's main concern. Examples printed: Hiding by Dorothy Aldis; The King's Breakfast by A. A. Milne; The Raven by E. A. Poe; The Cattle Thief by Pauline Johnson.","In poetry the language is molded into some kind of design. In prose it is not."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 3 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":["A portion of a longer poem may be chosen instead of the whole work.","Gestures may be used to improve the telling of the story."],"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A poem that tells a story and usually has dialogue. Telling the story is the speaker's main concern. Examples printed: Hiding by Dorothy Aldis; The King's Breakfast by A. A. Milne; The Raven by E. A. Poe; The Cattle Thief by Pauline Johnson.\\n- In poetry the language is molded into some kind of design. In prose it is not.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 3 minutes.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- A portion of a longer poem may be chosen instead of the whole work.\\n- Gestures may be used to improve the telling of the story."},{"Title":"Original Poetry","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["The participant performs their own original work, from memory.","In poetry the language is molded into some kind of design. In prose it is not."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 3 minutes."],"Rules":["The adjudicator must be given a copy of the poem."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- The participant performs their own original work, from memory.\\n- In poetry the language is molded into some kind of design. In prose it is not.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 3 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The adjudicator must be given a copy of the poem."},{"Title":"Memorized Prose","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A short selection of a few paragraphs from a novel or story that forms a dramatic unit. It is spoken in storytelling style, using the exact words of the author.","In prose the speaker is the narrator. The focus should be on the voice, with vocal characterization and quality of prose kept.","Gesture is encouraged, but movement around the stage should be very limited. Speakers may stand or be seated."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 5 minutes."],"Rules":["A balance of narration and dialogue is best."],"Allowed":["Editing is allowed where the quality of the prose can be kept."],"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A short selection of a few paragraphs from a novel or story that forms a dramatic unit. It is spoken in storytelling style, using the exact words of the author.\\n- In prose the speaker is the narrator. The focus should be on the voice, with vocal characterization and quality of prose kept.\\n- Gesture is encouraged, but movement around the stage should be very limited. Speakers may stand or be seated.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A balance of narration and dialogue is best.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Editing is allowed where the quality of the prose can be kept."},{"Title":"Literary Monologue","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A monologue, usually of the 'personal experience' type. It may be comic or serious: a speech from a diary, a letter, a solo monologue, or writing in the first person.","In prose the speaker is the narrator. The focus should be on the voice, with vocal characterization and quality of prose kept.","Gesture is encouraged, but movement around the stage should be very limited. Speakers may stand or be seated."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 5 minutes."],"Rules":["If the speech is part of a longer play, enter it in the Dramatic Solo class."],"Allowed":["Very limited movement or dramatization may be used.","Simple costumes and props may be used."],"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":["The festival's general costume rule allows costumes and props only in Drama and Shakespeare classes. This class page allows simple costumes and props."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A monologue, usually of the 'personal experience' type. It may be comic or serious: a speech from a diary, a letter, a solo monologue, or writing in the first person.\\n- In prose the speaker is the narrator. The focus should be on the voice, with vocal characterization and quality of prose kept.\\n- Gesture is encouraged, but movement around the stage should be very limited. Speakers may stand or be seated.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- If the speech is part of a longer play, enter it in the Dramatic Solo class.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Very limited movement or dramatization may be used.\\n- Simple costumes and props may be used.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The festival's general costume rule allows costumes and props only in Drama and Shakespeare classes. This class page allows simple costumes and props."},{"Title":"Storytelling","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["An original story, or the retelling of a known story, fairy tale, myth or legend.","In prose the speaker is the narrator. The focus should be on the voice, with vocal characterization and quality of prose kept.","Gesture is encouraged, but movement around the stage should be very limited. Speakers may stand or be seated."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 5 minutes."],"Rules":["Stories must be told, not read, and should sound spontaneous rather than memorized.","The storyteller should show the difference between characters and keep eye contact with the audience.","Competitors give only a brief outline of the key events of the story."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- An original story, or the retelling of a known story, fairy tale, myth or legend.\\n- In prose the speaker is the narrator. The focus should be on the voice, with vocal characterization and quality of prose kept.\\n- Gesture is encouraged, but movement around the stage should be very limited. Speakers may stand or be seated.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Stories must be told, not read, and should sound spontaneous rather than memorized.\\n- The storyteller should show the difference between characters and keep eye contact with the audience.\\n- Competitors give only a brief outline of the key events of the story."},{"Title":"Dramatic Solo","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["The selection holds the words of one character only; the words of other characters are left out.","In drama the speaker is the character portrayed. Simple props and costumes may be used. Blocking with motivated movement is encouraged."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 6 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":["Adaptations from stories are allowed if they can be performed dramatically as if part of a play.","Blocking, movement and simple staging suited to the scene are encouraged."],"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- The selection holds the words of one character only; the words of other characters are left out.\\n- In drama the speaker is the character portrayed. Simple props and costumes may be used. Blocking with motivated movement is encouraged.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 6 minutes.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Adaptations from stories are allowed if they can be performed dramatically as if part of a play.\\n- Blocking, movement and simple staging suited to the scene are encouraged."},{"Title":"Shakespeare Solo","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A speech of one character from a play by William Shakespeare. The solo is performed as if it were part of the whole play."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 5 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":["Lines of other characters may be cut, and a solo created, if the sense of the passage is kept."],"NotAllowed":["Selections from Shakespeare's non-dramatic poetry are not suitable for this class."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A speech of one character from a play by William Shakespeare. The solo is performed as if it were part of the whole play.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 5 minutes.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Lines of other characters may be cut, and a solo created, if the sense of the passage is kept.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Selections from Shakespeare's non-dramatic poetry are not suitable for this class."},{"Title":"Mime Solo","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A silent scene that tells a story through physical movement and expression."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 4 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":["Titles may be announced or shown on a sign.","A chair or bench may be used."],"NotAllowed":["No other props are allowed."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A silent scene that tells a story through physical movement and expression.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 4 minutes.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Titles may be announced or shown on a sign.\\n- A chair or bench may be used.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- No other props are allowed."},{"Title":"Public Speaking","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A prepared, structured speech on a topic chosen by the speaker. It is delivered in a deliberate way to inform, influence or entertain the listeners. Public speaking helps people organize and express what they know."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 6 minutes."],"Rules":["The style should be extemporaneous: well rehearsed but spontaneous sounding.","Speeches that are memorized or read from a manuscript are not encouraged.","An outline of the content of the speech must be given."],"Allowed":["Notes on cards may be used."],"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A prepared, structured speech on a topic chosen by the speaker. It is delivered in a deliberate way to inform, influence or entertain the listeners. Public speaking helps people organize and express what they know.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 6 minutes.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The style should be extemporaneous: well rehearsed but spontaneous sounding.\\n- Speeches that are memorized or read from a manuscript are not encouraged.\\n- An outline of the content of the speech must be given.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Notes on cards may be used."},{"Title":"Drama Duologue","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A scene for 2 characters taken from a published play or dramatized story. Each actor takes the language, movement and dress of the character.","In drama the speaker is the character portrayed. Simple props and costumes may be used. Blocking with motivated movement is encouraged."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 6 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A scene for 2 characters taken from a published play or dramatized story. Each actor takes the language, movement and dress of the character.\\n- In drama the speaker is the character portrayed. Simple props and costumes may be used. Blocking with motivated movement is encouraged.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 6 minutes."},{"Title":"Choral Speaking","Source":"PKMF Speech Arts Categories & Classes","Sections":{"About":["A group presentation of one or two selections of poetry or prose chosen to show contrast. The group works with a text, using the human voice as the instrument, with solo and massed voices of different colour and quality, and vocal sound-effects with dynamic effect."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time limit: 10 minutes."],"Rules":null,"Allowed":["Some solo and small-group voices may be included.","The chorus may use actions or gestures."],"NotAllowed":["No movement from place to place once the piece has begun.","A leader may conduct but may not speak."],"Judging":null,"Points":null,"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A group presentation of one or two selections of poetry or prose chosen to show contrast. The group works with a text, using the human voice as the instrument, with solo and massed voices of different colour and quality, and vocal sound-effects with dynamic effect.\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time limit: 10 minutes.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- Some solo and small-group voices may be included.\\n- The chorus may use actions or gestures.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- No movement from place to place once the piece has begun.\\n- A leader may conduct but may not speak."}]`),n=[],r=[{Key:"FeeNonCompPrimary",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo fee: Non-competitive Primary (6 years and under)",Value:"CAD 20.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeJunior",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo fee: Junior (7-13 years)",Value:"CAD 25.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeIntermediate",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo fee: Intermediate (14-17 years)",Value:"CAD 30.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeSenior",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo fee: Senior (18-22 years)",Value:"CAD 40.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeAdult",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo fee: Adult (23 years and over)",Value:"CAD 20.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeSeniors55",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo fee: Seniors (55 and over)",Value:"CAD 10.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeSightreading",Kind:"fee",Label:"Sightreading fee (all ages)",Value:"CAD 10.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeConcertGroup",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo recital class: Concert Group (age 7 and up)",Value:"CAD 60.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeStoryPublicSpeech",Kind:"fee",Label:"Solo recital class: Story + Public Speech",Value:"CAD 40.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeDuo",Kind:"fee",Label:"Group class fee: Duos (all ages)",Value:"CAD 20.00 per person",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeTrio",Kind:"fee",Label:"Group class fee: Trios (all ages)",Value:"CAD 20.00 per person",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeGroup",Kind:"fee",Label:"Group class fee: Groups of 4 or more (all ages)",Value:"CAD 60.00",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Ages, Fees & Time Limits"},{Key:"FeeCategoryChange",Kind:"fee",Label:"Fee for a category change request",Value:"CAD 15",Source:"PKMF General Rules & Regulations (How to enter, point 5)"},{Key:"ProtestFee",Kind:"fee",Label:"Protest fee",Value:"CAD 25.00 (returned in full if the protest is upheld)",Source:"PKMF General Rules & Regulations (Adjudication, point 4)"},{Key:"RegistrationOpens",Kind:"date",Label:"Registration opens (printed for the 2024 festival)",Value:"2023-12-01",Source:"PKMF General Rules & Regulations (printed 'Opens December 1, 2023')"},{Key:"RegistrationCloses",Kind:"date",Label:"Registration closes (printed for the 2024 festival)",Value:"2024-01-29",Source:"PKMF General Rules & Regulations (printed 'Closes January 29, 2024')"},{Key:"FeesDueWithin",Kind:"text",Label:"When entry fees are due",Value:"Within 30 days of the registration closing",Source:"PKMF General Rules & Regulations (How to enter)"},{Key:"RegistrationNotice",Kind:"news",Label:"Registration notice on the festival website",Value:[{Date:"",Title:"Registration information",Text:"Information will be posted in September. The dates apply to all disciplines.",Url:""}],Source:"PKMF Registration page"},{Key:"ScholarshipDeadline",Kind:"date",Label:"Tri-Discipline Scholarship application deadline",Value:"April 1 of the festival year",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Scholarships & Awards; PKMF Scholarships page"},{Key:"PastSpeechArtsDates",Kind:"text",Label:"Past edition: Speech and Dramatic Arts dates (2018)",Value:"Wednesday 11 and Thursday 12 April 2018",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Home (an old edition block still printed on the page)"},{Key:"PastSpeechArtsVenue",Kind:"address",Label:"Past edition: Speech and Dramatic Arts venue (2018)",Value:{Name:"Leir House",Address:"",City:"Penticton, BC",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Home (an old edition block, 2018)"},{Key:"PastProvincialsPABC",Kind:"text",Label:"Past edition: Performing Arts BC Festival (2025)",Value:"1 to 5 June 2025, Victoria, BC",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Provincials"},{Key:"PastProvincialsCanWest",Kind:"text",Label:"Past edition: Canada West Festival (2025)",Value:"24 to 26 July 2025, Saskatoon",Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Provincials"},{Key:"SpeechArtsCaptain",Kind:"contact",Label:"Speech Arts discipline captain",Value:{Name:"Debbie McGladdery",Role:"Speech and Dramatic Arts Captain",Phone:"250-492-1126",WhatsApp:"",Email:""},Source:"PKMF Speech Arts Home; PKMF Contact page"},{Key:"FestivalOffice",Kind:"contact",Label:"Festival office",Value:{Name:"Penticton Kiwanis Music, Dance and Speech Arts Festival",Role:"Office",Phone:"250-493-8322",WhatsApp:"",Email:"pkmfoffice@gmail.com"},Source:"PKMF Contact page; PKMF Scholarships page"},{Key:"ExecutiveDirector",Kind:"contact",Label:"Festival Executive Director",Value:{Name:"Judi Ritcey",Role:"Executive Director",Phone:"250-493-8322",WhatsApp:"",Email:""},Source:"PKMF Contact page; About PKMF"},{Key:"FestivalMailingAddress",Kind:"address",Label:"Festival mailing address",Value:{Name:"Penticton Kiwanis Music Festival (PKMF)",Address:"Box 23041",City:"Penticton, BC V2A 8L7",MapUrl:"",Directions:""},Source:"PKMF Contact page; PKMF Speech Arts Scholarships & Awards"},{Key:"FestivalBoard",Kind:"text",Label:"Festival board executive (as printed)",Value:"President: Jeanette Palfrey. Treasurer: Joanne Purser. Recording Secretary: Joanne Forsyth. Executive Director: Judi Ritcey. Speech and Dramatic Arts Captain: Debbie McGladdery.",Source:"PKMF About page"}],l=[{Claim:"Classes, descriptions, time limits, ages, fees, rules, awards and provincial requirements are those printed on the Penticton Kiwanis Music Festival website (read from Internet Archive snapshots of 2025).",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"Penticton Kiwanis Music, Dance and Speech Arts Festival, Speech Arts Categories & Classes; Ages, Fees & Time Limits; Rules & Regulations; Provincials; Awards (web.archive.org snapshots, 2025)",Url:"http://www.pkmf.org/speech-arts-syllabus.aspx"},{Claim:"The current edition's dates, venue and adjudicator are not printed; the website says registration information will be posted in September. Past-edition facts are Variables marked with their year.",SourceType:"Primary - the organiser's own website",Source:"Penticton Kiwanis Music Festival, Registration page (web.archive.org, July 2025)",Url:"http://www.pkmf.org/registration.aspx"}],c={TemplateKey:e,Year:2025,Note:t,Page:o,ParticipantTypes:a,AgeGroups:i,Competitions:s,CompetitionAgeGroups:n,Variables:r,Evidence:l};export{i as AgeGroups,n as CompetitionAgeGroups,s as Competitions,l as Evidence,t as Note,o as Page,a as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,r as Variables,d as Year,c as default};
